  "body": "**Fixes #717.** Adds an MCP `repair_model` tool, so a headless session can close an imported mesh's open edges. Until now, only the Model page's Repair button could do that.\n\n**Stacked on #719** (#716, `move_model`): both add to the new `crates/app/src/mcp/model_tools.rs` and `crates/app/tests/mcp_model.rs`. This PR's own change is the last commit, b595b24e. Please merge #719 first; this PR will then merge `main` and show only its own diff.\n\n### What changed\n\n- **`crates/app/src/mcp/model_tools.rs`:** the `repair_model { name, weld_tol?, max_hole_edges? }` tool.\n  - It builds the GUI's own `RepairModel` and runs it through the document History: one undo step that restores the exact previous facets.\n  - The defaults are the Model page's, 0.001 mm and 256 edges (`RepairUi::default`), kept as named constants.\n  - The reply carries every `RepairReport` count (vertices and triangles before and after, `welded`, `dropped`, `holes_filled`, `triangles_added`) and the command's one-line `summary`.\n  - The repair runs at construction, so a refused repair never reaches the history. Every refusal is the command's own text: an unknown name, setup furniture, a negative tolerance, a budget under 3 edges, a repair refusal, and \"nothing to repair\". The tool itself only refuses a value of the wrong JSON type, by name.\n- **Decision record:** `docs/decisions/717-mcp-repair-model.md`.\n\n### Pinning tests (`crates/app/tests/mcp_model.rs`)\n\n- `repair_model_fills_an_open_boxs_missing_face_in_one_undo_step`: an STL box with its top face left out has 10 facets. The repair reports 1 hole filled with 2 triangles (10 to 12), the defaults 0.001 and 256, and \"filled 1 hole\". Undo brings back the 10 facets and redo the 12. A second repair is refused with \"nothing to repair\".\n- `repair_model_refuses_by_name_and_a_refusal_enters_no_history`: each case is refused by name: a closed box (\"nothing to repair\"), an unknown name, `weld_tol: -0.1`, `max_hole_edges: 2`, `max_hole_edges: 2.5`, a string tolerance, a missing name. Nothing changes, and the next `undo` still undoes the last import.\n\nOn `main` both fail at their first `repair_model` call, because the tool doesn't exist there.\n\n### What ran (Windows 10, local)\n\nFork: `austin62jake/Camcraft2`, `main` at upstream f42d3788 (contains v0.16.0, PROCESS_VERSION 4). Gated on b595b24e.\n\n- **Quick gate plan** (`scripts/gate.ps1 -Tier quick -DryRun`, run by hand at `-j 6`): no failures, nothing ignored.\n  - App lib: 103 passed.\n  - App suites: `contour_arc_sim` 1, `mcp` 13, `mcp_live` 10, `mcp_model` 5, `mcp_pick` 2, `mcp_pockets` 3, `mcp_render` 5, `mcp_setup` 4, `wall_column_gouge` 3.\n  - UI `models`: 4 passed.\n- **Clippy:** `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`, clean.\n- **Not run:** Linux, macOS, the batch tier. The existing command-level suite `repair_model` isn't in the plan either, because `RepairModel` is unchanged. `camcraft-cli mcp` over stdio wasn't tried; the tests drive the same `Server::handle` in-process.\n\nWith this PR, the `camcraft-mcp` skill's gap 3 (`RepairModel`) is done too. The skill is left alone for the same reason given in #719: editing it is a process change, with a `PROCESS_VERSION` bump.\n\nChangelog: The MCP server can repair an imported model's mesh (`repair_model`): weld, drop degenerate triangles and fill open loops, with the counts reported back (#717)\n\nFixes #717\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
